    {
     "type": "changes",
     "title": "Your comments",
     "groups": [
      {
       "section": "Navbar",
       "items": [
        {
         "ask": "1. On mobile landscape it should not disappear when the hero is visible.",
         "done": "It stays while any of the hero is under it: it starts to move only when the hero's bottom edge reaches it, and then goes up with that edge (<code>nav.js</code>, <code>initHide</code>).",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "2. It should appear and disappear more smoothly.",
         "done": "It now follows the scroll px for px, like Safari's own bars, and when the scrolling stops it settles with a glide that keeps its speed (0.42s, the site's arrive curve): back if it was last moving back, away if it was moving away. With reduce motion it fades as before.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "3. Scrolling up in mobile Safari it appears with a jump: down in place, then up, then down again.",
         "done": "Most likely cause: when Safari's bars come back on the way up, the page's height and scroll position change by themselves for a moment, and 2px of that counted as scrolling down, which hid the navbar again. Now nothing the browser does by itself moves it: for 0.4s after the visible screen changes size it does not follow the scroll, nor while the page is pulled past its ends; and since it follows the finger, a small slip would move it only by that much. I could not run Safari here: please check on your iPhone.",
         "tag": "check"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "section": "Hero",
       "items": [
        {
         "ask": "1. Min and max values, so it won't stretch or shrink too much; analyse how it should look on different screens.",
         "done": "Each part has a least and a greatest size: the team at least 168 → 300px (by the width alone), at most 40.8% of the width or 540; the air above the text at least 32 → 64, below 40 → 72, at most 64 → 128 each. The section is the screen less the gap, but never taller than its parts at their greatest (so 390×844 is 574 instead of 826, 2560×1440 is 1112 instead of 1392) and never shorter than its parts at their least (it then runs past the fold). New roles in <code>design/scale.json</code>; the table and the chart below have every size.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "2. The space above and below the info block should be generous.",
         "done": "At least 32/40px on the smallest screens and 64/72 on large ones (was 16/16 → 48/32); the room left over is shared 4 : 1 : 1 between the team and the two airs.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "3. Around 620px tall and lower the picture becomes too small.",
         "done": "The team keeps its least size on short screens (291 at 1366×650, 281 at 1280×620); the hero is then a little taller than the screen, so on those laptops the first card's pill is below the fold. Say if you would rather keep the pill on the first screen: a smaller least size brings it back.",
         "tag": "differently"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "section": "Cards",
       "items": [
        {
         "ask": "1. Sometimes the card's colour shows as a line around the overlapping white element.",
         "done": "Found and measured: the white strips between cards were drawn from four background tiles, and on ×2/×3 screens the seams between the tiles let the card's colour through (28 of 90 frames at 375×667 ×3). Each strip is now one shape: a hole with the card's corners, its shadow the page colour. Also the white label of a finished card no longer lays a white copy over the label's own border edge. 0 frames now.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "2. On horizontal layouts, centre the illo in the available space.",
         "done": "The text column is as wide as the titles need (12.5 of their letters and the padding), and the drawing is centred in all the card right of it (at most as tall as the card).",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "3. The first illo not cut by the card: appears at about half the card, grows to the available size; the last one shrinks back and disappears.",
         "done": "The drawing follows the open part of its card: it is as big as that part leaves room for and centred in it; it appears when half the card is open and grows to its full size, and the last one shrinks with the closing card and goes at half. Only a transform moves (no layout), with <code>will-change</code> just while it moves.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "4. Text from blur.",
         "done": "The titles come out of a blur (0.22em) with a short rise (18% of a line) and go into one. The blur is cleared once a title has arrived.",
         "tag": "done"
        },
        {
         "ask": "5. A card shrunk to a pill stays pinned for a bit, then scrolls away by a trigger; scrolling back it does not re-pin.",
         "done": "On the way down a copy of the pill stays fixed under the navbar while the next card comes up under it, and leaves (slides up and fades, 0.5s) when the next card's title would reach it. Scrolled back, it leaves at once and the real card comes back with the page. Not for the last card. How long it stays is the open question: until the title reaches it is about 80–140px of scroll; staying longer would cover the next title (it would then have to wait).",
         "tag": "check"
        },
        {
         "ask": "6. Sometimes cards appear overlapping and stay under the next section (see the image).",
         "done": "The images did not come through, and I could not reproduce it (reloads mid-stack, rotations, jumps, fast scrolling, 6 sizes). Done on the way: the sections after the stack are now drawn over it explicitly (z-index 1). Please send the screenshots and where it happened (phone, browser).",
         "tag": "open"
        },
        {
         "ask": "7. The title should disappear a bit earlier when the card is about to close.",
         "done": "A closing card (going away, or scrolled back as it came) hides its title 12% of the card's height before the edge would reach it, and the title leaves into a blur.",
         "tag": "done"
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "list",
     "title": "What it changes that you might not expect",
     "items": [
      "<b>Short laptops lose the pill.</b> Below about 690px of height (1366×650, 1280×620) the hero is taller than the screen, so the first card's pill is no longer on the first screen; the bottom of the table is cut by the fold.",
      "<b>Tall screens show more of the first card.</b> On a tall phone, a tablet upright and big monitors the hero stops growing, and the first card shows under it with its label (390×844: 270px of it).",
      "<b>Sideways phones scroll before the team shows whole.</b> 844×390: the hero is 508px, the team's heads are at the bottom of the first screen.",
      "<b>The pinned pill covers the top of the next card's label</b> while that card comes up under it (the label slides under the pill).",
      "<b>The drawings move while a card opens and closes</b> (scale and position, each frame): on a weak phone this is the most work the stack does per frame now. Watched with <code>will-change</code> only while moving."
     ]
    }
   ]
